WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.250
Bread for dinner comes from the bakery round the corner, ordered by phone.

2
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.875
It's a lovely bakery with a terrible phone.

3
00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:14.209
Sometimes the call drops halfway, and sometimes nobody picks up at all.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.030
So the cook has a routine.

5
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.477
If the bread doesn't come, she serves crackers instead.

6
00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:23.739
Or she rings again, after a short pause, and a longer one each time.

7
00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:28.315
And meanwhile, plates can drop anywhere: in the kitchen, or at the table.

8
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:32.882
Here's the bread course as a flow, a recipe card for values.

9
00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:35.552
The cook first sends out yesterday's loaf.

10
00:00:35.552 --> 00:00:42.228
Then she rings the bakery inside withTimeout of 50 milliseconds, which gives up if the call takes longer.

11
00:00:42.228 --> 00:00:47.188
Below her sits catch, a helper that sends out crackers if anything goes wrong.

12
00:00:47.188 --> 00:00:49.985
And today the bakery takes 100 milliseconds.

13
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.274
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

14
00:00:53.274 --> 00:01:00.966
A timeout is a kind of cancellation: a CancellationException, a coroutine's way of saying, stop, you've been called off.

15
00:01:00.966 --> 00:01:03.851
And catch doesn't handle cancellation, right?

16
00:01:03.851 --> 00:01:07.120
So doesn't the timeout fly straight past the catch?

17
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:10.860
It doesn't.

18
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.878
The diner gets yesterday's loaf, then crackers, and the caller sees nothing.

19
00:01:15.878 --> 00:01:17.727
The catch heard the timeout.

20
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:22.581
So the rule we all learned, catch ignores cancellation, is a little too tidy.

21
00:01:22.581 --> 00:01:26.989
Let's find the real one, by ending the bread course five different ways.

22
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:35.207
The bakery call throws an IOException, Java's usual exception for a failed call, like a dropped line.

23
00:01:35.207 --> 00:01:35.890
Crackers.

24
00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.081
The bakery throws a CancellationException.

25
00:01:39.081 --> 00:01:40.221
Crackers again.

26
00:01:40.221 --> 00:01:41.588
The timeout fires.

27
00:01:41.588 --> 00:01:42.272
Crackers.

28
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.238
Now the diner drops a plate.

29
00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:50.437
The catch does nothing, and the caller sees an IllegalStateException: the diner dropped the plate.

30
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.206
And last, the caller's own job is cancelled while it waits.

31
00:01:54.206 --> 00:01:58.912
The catch does nothing again, and the caller sees a JobCancellationException.

32
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.220
Here's the picture.

33
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.254
A net is strung under the cook's side of the line.

34
00:02:03.254 --> 00:02:07.867
Everything above catch is called the upstream: the cook and her bakery call.

35
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:13.451
Everything below it is the downstream: the diner, the collector, and whoever called collect.

36
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:20.026
Anything that falls from above lands in the net, even a cancellation, as long as the job is still running.

37
00:02:20.026 --> 00:02:24.149
A plate the diner drops falls below the net, straight to the caller.

38
00:02:24.149 --> 00:02:30.879
And when the caller calls off the whole dinner, the net steps aside, on purpose, so that nothing keeps cooking.

39
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:34.842
But an exception doesn't carry a label saying who threw it.

40
00:02:34.842 --> 00:02:36.314
So how does catch know?

41
00:02:36.314 --> 00:02:38.489
Let's build it by hand, in Kotlin.

42
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:44.378
First it wraps the downstream emit, and remembers anything that comes out of it.

43
00:02:44.378 --> 00:02:46.487
That's the diner's dropped plate.

44
00:02:46.487 --> 00:02:50.192
Then, when the whole collect fails, it asks two questions.

45
00:02:50.192 --> 00:02:51.790
Did the diner throw this?

46
00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:52.556
Throw it on.

47
00:02:52.556 --> 00:02:54.984
Is it the cancellation of our own job?

48
00:02:54.984 --> 00:02:56.135
Throw that on too.

49
00:02:56.135 --> 00:02:58.435
Everything else goes to your action.

50
00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:04.885
Remember the inspector from lesson 2, who refuses any emit after the diner has thrown?

51
00:03:04.885 --> 00:03:07.894
That rule has a name: exception transparency.

52
00:03:07.894 --> 00:03:11.305
An exception from downstream belongs to downstream.

53
00:03:11.305 --> 00:03:15.117
So catch must never swallow it and serve crackers on top.

54
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.626
The net is generous, but it only covers what hangs above it.

55
00:03:19.626 --> 00:03:22.177
Serving inside collect is below the net.

56
00:03:22.177 --> 00:03:28.236
To protect it, move the serving into onEach, above catch, and call collect with nothing inside.

57
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.471
And a caught error still ends the upstream.

58
00:03:31.471 --> 00:03:36.613
map fails on 2, catch emits minus 1, and the diner gets 1, then minus 1.

59
00:03:36.613 --> 00:03:38.041
The 3 is never made.

60
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.342
So here's the rule.

61
00:03:39.342 --> 00:03:41.867
catch hears only what falls from above it.

62
00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:44.151
Put the work it should cover above it.

63
00:03:44.867 --> 00:03:46.895
The net can send out crackers.

64
00:03:46.895 --> 00:03:49.938
Sometimes you'd rather ring the bakery again.

65
00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:50.817
That's retry.

66
00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.937
The grocer's van brings a delivery note: flour, eggs and butter, read line by line.

67
00:03:56.937 --> 00:04:01.274
On the first try, the line drops after eggs, with an IOException.

68
00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:04.276
The pantry reads the note through retry of 3.

69
00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:06.211
What does the pantry receive?

70
00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:11.806
Flour, eggs, flour, eggs, butter.

71
00:04:11.806 --> 00:04:13.653
Two lines went in twice.

72
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.105
A retry collects the upstream again.

73
00:04:16.105 --> 00:04:21.419
And a cold flow is a recipe card: collecting it again cooks it again, from the top.

74
00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:24.108
Values that already went out go out again.

75
00:04:24.108 --> 00:04:28.461
So retry of 3 means three more tries, up to four collections in all.

76
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.125
The flour doesn't mind.

77
00:04:30.125 --> 00:04:34.567
The pantry book does: it now says we bought twice the flour we did.

78
00:04:34.567 --> 00:04:38.943
So whatever comes after a retry must be safe to see an item twice.

79
00:04:38.943 --> 00:04:40.600
That's called idempotent.

80
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.313
For example, skip any line whose id you've already seen.

81
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.548
Now the cook's pauses.

82
00:04:46.548 --> 00:04:51.966
Ringing a bakery that won't answer a thousand times a second isn't a routine.

83
00:04:51.966 --> 00:04:53.232
It's a prank call.

84
00:04:53.232 --> 00:04:54.499
So the waits grow.

85
00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:56.951
That growing wait is a backoff.

86
00:04:56.951 --> 00:05:01.741
In code, it's a delay inside retryWhen, the operator under retry.

87
00:05:01.741 --> 00:05:10.068
It gets the cause, and an attempt number that starts at 0, so the waits are 100 milliseconds, then 200, then 400.

88
00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:17.018
Against a bakery that never answers, she rings at 0, 100, 300 and 700 milliseconds.

89
00:05:17.018 --> 00:05:21.736
Then she gives up, and the original IOException comes out.

90
00:05:21.933 --> 00:05:24.692
Reactor's backoff rings at the same times.

91
00:05:24.692 --> 00:05:33.035
But it wraps the last error in a RetryExhaustedException, and outside tests it adds jitter, a small random spread on each wait.

92
00:05:33.035 --> 00:05:37.633
Flow has no jitter and no cap built in: you write the policy yourself.

93
00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:40.885
Position matters for the egg timer too.

94
00:05:40.885 --> 00:05:43.633
A timeout above the retry limits each try.

95
00:05:43.633 --> 00:05:47.036
Below it, a timeout limits them all, waits included.

96
00:05:47.733 --> 00:05:49.899
So, does a timeout fly past catch?

97
00:05:49.899 --> 00:05:50.090
No.

98
00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.893
catch doesn't sort exceptions by their type.

99
00:05:52.893 --> 00:05:54.039
It asks who threw.

100
00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:58.371
What falls from the cook's side lands in the net, timeouts included.

101
00:05:58.371 --> 00:06:02.447
The diner's dropped plate, and the caller's own cancel, go past.

102
00:06:02.447 --> 00:06:05.377
And a retry doesn't carry on where it stopped.

103
00:06:05.377 --> 00:06:07.925
It cooks the recipe again, from the top.

104
00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.006
Quick check.

105
00:06:09.006 --> 00:06:11.904
catch sits above map, and map throws.

106
00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:13.079
Who handles it?

107
00:06:16.133 --> 00:06:17.157
Not that catch.

108
00:06:17.157 --> 00:06:21.800
map is below the net, so its exception falls past it, to the caller.
